\chapter{The game engine}
\label{ch:game}

The Overview promised a core that knows the rules but never speaks to the
screen (\cref{ch:overview}).  This chapter delivers the smallest such core:
the boundary every frontend will translate across.  It has three moving
parts, and the chapter is really the relationship between them:

\begin{description}
  \item[Commands] name what the player wants to do, stripped of how they
    said it --- the input side of the boundary.
  \item[Events] name what happened as a result, stripped of how it will be
    shown --- the output side.
  \item[The engine] is the function between the two: it accepts a command
    and returns the events that command produced.
\end{description}

Because the engine speaks only these two vocabularies, the terminal and web
frontends can be thin translators over one engine rather than two divergent
programs: each turns keystrokes or clicks into Commands and renders Events as
text or HTML.  At this milestone the world is still empty --- locations,
items and maps arrive in later chapters --- so the engine can do little.
What it must do correctly \emph{now} is establish the
\emph{command\,$\to$\,events} contract and the dispatch shape that every
later command plugs into.%
\ltnote{%
  We define the Command and Event families before the engine that maps between
  them.  The engine's contract only reads clearly once both its input and output
  vocabularies are in hand, so the two data types come first and the function
  between them last.
}

We assemble the module from named buckets, as the package root does.  The
engine needs the first [[imports]] and [[classes]] buckets, so they appear
here; the parser later in this chapter also brings module-level [[functions]]
and a [[verb registry]], which we list in the assembly now and fill where each
is explained.  The [[functions]] precede the [[classes]] and the registry
follows them, mirroring the Locations chapter (\cref{ch:locations}): a function
calls the classes only at run time, while the registry names them as it is
built, so it must tangle after they exist.  The module docstring restates the
one rule for a reader who opens [[game.py]] without the woven document beside
it.

<<[[game.py]]>>=
"""The presentation-agnostic game core.

Defines the Command and Event types, the parser that turns a line of text
into a Command, and the Engine that maps commands to events.  This module
imports no user interface and calls neither ``print`` nor ``input``: a
frontend parses player input into a Command, hands it to the Engine, and
renders the Events the Engine returns.
"""

